Experiment 9(b): I/O Task Simulation and Memory Mapping

Steps:
Open the Ubuntu terminal.
Create the C program using nano expt9b.c.
Write the C program to simulate an I/O task by repeatedly writing data to a file using fopen(), fprintf(), and fclose().
Save the file using Ctrl + O, press Enter, and exit using Ctrl + X.
Compile the program using gcc expt9b.c -o expt9b.
Execute the program using ./expt9b.
Observe the I/O task messages displayed on the terminal.
Press Ctrl + C to terminate the program.
Execute the top command to monitor system resource utilization. Press q to exit.
Display the contents of the generated file using cat dummy.txt.
Run the program again using ./expt9b and keep it running.
Open another terminal and identify the process ID using pgrep -a expt9b.
Examine the memory mappings using pmap -x PID, replacing PID with the actual process ID.
Press Ctrl + C in the terminal running the program to terminate it.

Result: An I/O-intensive task was simulated using file operations, system resource utilization was monitored using top, and process memory mappings were examined using pmap -x.
